How did development actually happen? How can poor societies escape the chicken-and-egg problem of development: growth or good governance first?&nbsp;
<div>
 Yuen Yuen Ang challenges linear, Western-centric theories of development-the Industrial-Colonial Paradigm-which are trapped in the false debate over whether growth or good governance should come first and assume that "good governance" must take forms that exist in the West.
 <div>
  Using China’s capitalist revolution from the 1980s to 2012 as her primary demonstration site, Ang advances a paradigm shift toward a systems-based, pluralistic, and reflexive political economy—what she now formalizes as AIM (Adaptive, Inclusive, Moral).&nbsp;
 </div>
 <div>
  &nbsp;Applying AIM, Ang shows that China’s transformation was not driven by centralized control but by Directed Improvisation—direction from Beijing paired with bottom-up improvisation among local governments. This system of adaptive governance enabled a coevolutionary process of development: local actors began by harnessing normatively weak institutions to build markets— “using what they have”—before later establishing modern institutions to preserve markets.&nbsp;
 </div>
 <div>
  As a paradigm-building book, How China Escaped the Poverty Trap introduced a family of interconnected concepts, theories, and methods: Complex vs. Complicated; Influence vs. Control; Institutions vs. Meta-institutions; Directed Improvisation; Coevolutionary&nbsp;
 </div>
